The fixed selection covers 14 subjects and 25 batches of eight, with unchanged historical five-shot
CoT prompts, thinking disabled in the chat template, greedy decoding, seed 42, EOS enabled, maximum
8192 output tokens, model length 16384, and existing collection bounds of 215300 coordinator
steps/10800 seconds. The longest prompt plus cap is 10687 tokens. Preserve seed-first admission
after the first actual scheduler output token, then seven peers; drain between batches. There are no
answer retries or output-based scheduling substitutions.

These are **in-process engine API terminal responses** from ordinary `LLM`/`InprocClient`, not HTTP
responses. The frozen driver stores each real `RequestOutput.finished` response once, verifies
prompt IDs and single-sample output, and records `stop` or `length`. `finished=True` means the API
request ended; it does not mean a correct or untruncated answer.

Two fresh native v2 controls and each candidate must use the same pinned Core/Ascend/plugin executed
sources, exact existing dirty-state hashes, driver bytes, model/tokenizer identity, recorded
environment, and all common engine arguments. Explicit shared settings are
`HCCL_DETERMINISTIC=true`, `enable_npugraph_ex=false`, `enable_static_kernel=false`, with
`VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` unset. Plugin commit is `f7ed21e183b27d2141176f3e7a5c8a5ff4ede07d`. Declared
worker/scheduler and native-chunked versus raw/group unchunked behavior differ by design; all other
existing provenance checks remain mandatory. The two controls require distinct fresh process
identities/times. Do not use v1 answers, an old compiler configuration, or a different plugin
revision as a v2 control. Group also requires a complete, comparable raw reference on the same
source/runtime/plan.

## Proposed supplement: checks fixed before candidate collection

1. **Complete response collection is a separate prerequisite.** Each native control and candidate
   must have exactly one terminal result for every one of the 200 frozen IDs, no extra or duplicate
   IDs, no runtime/API errors, `collection_complete=true`, `shutdown_returned=true`, and a
   successful terminal controller/cleanup receipt. Each stored response must be finished and have an
   allowed terminal reason (`stop` or `length`), comply with the frozen budget, and pass
   source/input/output bindings. A length response is allowed to satisfy this collection
   prerequisite only; it still scores wrong. Pending requests, missing answers, unexpected
   termination reasons, invalid provenance, or incomplete shutdown cannot yield a supplementary
   pass. Missing cases count wrong in any partial descriptive score with denominator 200, **and
   additionally fail collection**. Do not infer successful collection from an accuracy number.
1. **Keep per-case correctness identical to the original strict scorer.** Set `correct_i=1` only
   when the frozen original scorer reports a finished, error-free `stop` response, an in-range
   letter from the unchanged canonical extraction function, and equality to the frozen public gold.
   Otherwise set `correct_i=0`. All `length` responses are wrong even if text contains a
   gold-matching letter. Invalid extraction, out-of-range letter, error, missing and nonterminal
   responses are wrong. Do not extract a previously ignored answer from a truncated response to gain
   credit. Denominator is always 200, never the number of valid, returned, natural-stop or
   stable-native cases.
1. **Keep the native-stable veto unchanged.** Let `S` be the frozen selected IDs with `correct_i=1`
   in both fresh native controls, using exactly the above per-case rule. Require `S` nonempty and no
   candidate error on any member of `S`. One new wrong answer fails; gains elsewhere do not
   compensate this veto.
1. **Keep the aggregate floor unchanged.** Require candidate total correct to be at least the lower
   of the two native correct counts. This floor allows compensation only outside the native-stable
   veto; it is not a claim that every individual case outside `S` is protected.
1. **Retain actual mixed-work coverage separately.** Replay hashed scheduler/retirement receipts
   with the existing validator: each of the 25 candidate batches must contain actual P layer
   progress and D retirement in one transaction. Missing batches fail the supplementary combined
   workload claim even if answer checks pass. Preserve the separate raw-versus-group paired
   transitions and raw-source receipt; report them even when either native-based candidate check
   fails. This proposal does not silently add a new raw-stable veto, and does not silently drop the
   existing coverage requirement.
1. **Report strict and supplementary results with distinct field names.** Preserve original scorer
   outputs byte for byte. Suggested new fields are
   `supplementary_budgeted_mcq_v1_collection_passed`,
   `supplementary_budgeted_mcq_v1_answer_nonregression_passed`, and
   `supplementary_budgeted_mcq_v1_mixed_scope_passed`. The last requires collection, veto, floor,
   nonempty `S`, provenance and mixed coverage. Never write a true value to the existing
   `bounded_answer_quality_gate_passed` or `full_frozen_plan_passed` on the strength of the
   supplement. The original v2 no-truncation condition remains false even if the supplement passes.

Implementation should reuse the original scorer's verified `rows`, per-case flags and
paired-statistics functions without modifying its `score`/`evaluate` behavior. Put the new
collection predicate and names in a new, separately hashed offline scorer. Before any candidate
results, CPU tests should demonstrate: a `length` response with a gold letter is still wrong; 199
valid responses plus one missing cannot pass collection; duplicate/unknown/error responses cannot
pass; strict gate remains false while the separate supplement can pass a synthetic fully collected
equal-score example; a single new wrong within `S` vetoes offsetting gains; the lower-native floor
and nonempty-set guard remain; source/plan mismatches reject; missing mixed receipts prevent the
workload-scoped pass. This review changes no producer or frozen file.

## Canonical adapter context and reporting limitations

Primary local scoring evidence is `frozen-v2/canonical-adapter.py`, copied and hashed from canonical
benchmark checkout `55181feb086af61c1cb953225ebe7e70167f77bb`. Its header attributes prompt
formatting and extraction to `TIGER-AI-Lab/MMLU-Pro`, Apache-2.0, `evaluate_from_local.py` at
revision `f418b116db00b065c2aea046518d8fcf74d39872`; the dataset is pinned to revision
`b189ec765aa7ed75c8acfea42df31fdae71f97be`. `QUALITY-PLAN.md` and the frozen source-row/gold hashes
document reconstruction of the same materialized prompts and 200-ID selection. This review uses that
local source, not an unverified current upstream evaluator.

The extraction function tries the `answer is` pattern, then `Answer:`/`answer:`, then the last
standalone A–J letter. Invalid/out-of-range results have no random-guess fallback. The **canonical
adapter's aggregate `score_results` does not itself reject every length-limited successful
response**: it can parse that text and records `length_limited` separately. Our existing strict
scorer and this supplement deliberately apply the stricter rule that every length output is wrong.
Thus describe this as a bounded protocol using the canonical prompt/gold/parser with conservative
budget-failure scoring, not an unmodified canonical aggregate score or full MMLU-Pro leaderboard
result. The canonical adapter's `MAX_OUTPUT=2048` also does not redefine the separately frozen v2
request budget.

Publish all 200 case statuses, correct counts/accuracy, length/invalid/error/missing counts and IDs,
total returned responses, all four paired transitions versus each native control, native/native
transitions, group/raw transitions, `S` size and new-wrong IDs, plus actual mixed-work coverage.
Separate length-limited failures from natural-stop invalid answers; do not hide length cases only
inside a combined invalid count. Retain answer/token outputs and exact source bindings. Any partial
report must identify pending cases and must not call the supplement passed.

Reuse the original descriptive exact accuracy intervals, paired difference intervals and stable-set
new-error upper bound with their assumptions disclosed. The selected subset is fixed rather than a
random population sample; a nonsignificant McNemar test does not prove equivalence. Zero observed
regressions on `S` is limited by the size and selection of `S`, and excludes cases where either
control was already wrong. Budgeted answer accuracy can measure a useful task outcome, but it cannot
establish numerical identity, universal semantic correctness, tail reliability, throughput, or the
reason native output looped/truncated. Truncation count/latency distributions remain important
adverse observations even when native and candidate are equally wrong.

The conclusion, if all separate checks later pass, should be limited to: “Under the frozen
8192-token budget and common deterministic/fusion configuration, the candidate returned all 200
selected responses, introduced no wrong answer among cases both native controls answered correctly,
and met the lower-native correct-count floor; original no-truncation acceptance failed.” This is
supplementary bounded evidence. Numerical/state invariants, pending-ACK/KV cancellation and reuse,
same-source raw/group controls, and uninstrumented performance/value evidence remain separate
outstanding PR10/Issue #11/#23 obligations.
